.TH OB-UNINSTALL 8 "September 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-uninstall \- take Open Bastion off this host, ready for package removal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-uninstall
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-y | \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-revoke ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ob-uninstall
un\-configures a host set up by
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8)
under any of its names and roles
.RB ( ob\-bastion\-setup ,
.BR ob\-standalone\-setup ,
.BR ob\-backend\-setup ),
and leaves it ready for
.B apt purge open\-bastion
or
.BR "dnf remove open\-bastion" .
.PP
Removing the package alone is not a way back. It does not know about the
setup's backups, so on a set\-up host it leaves the sshd drop\-in, whose
.B ForceCommand
names a session recorder that no longer exists, and a PAM stack that
.I requires
a module that no longer exists: every SSH login is refused. It also leaves
the NSS source, the sudoers rule, the KRL and audit schedules and a server
token that the portal still honours.
.PP
.B ob-uninstall
first prints what it is going to do, then asks. It decides everything before
changing anything, so a run that has to refuse does so before its first
write.
.SH WHAT IT UNDOES
In this order. The point of the order is that at no moment is an LLNG
certificate accepted without the checks that normally vet it: sshd stops
trusting the certificate authority
.I before
the PAM stack stops asking the portal.
.TP
.B 1. sshd
.IR sshd_config.d/00\-open\-bastion\-bastion.conf ,
.IR 00\-open\-bastion\-backend.conf ,
their legacy
.I 50\-
names;
.I 09\-open\-bastion\-service\-keys.conf
and
.I 60\-max\-security.conf
only when they carry the Open Bastion header. On a host without
.IR sshd_config.d ,
where the setup appended its blocks to
.IR sshd_config ,
that file is restored from the oldest setup backup that does not contain
them; failing that (a single Mode E run keeps only a copy that already has
the first block), the blocks are cut out along the exact lines the setup
wrote, and the rest of the file is kept. If a block does not have the
expected shape, or anything of Open Bastion's remains once they are cut, the
command refuses. Then
.BR "sshd \-t" :
if sshd rejects its configuration, the sshd files are put back and the
command stops with everything else untouched. Otherwise the CA key and
.I /usr/local/sbin/ob\-ssh\-principals
are removed (each only when no remaining sshd file refers to it) and sshd is
reloaded, with
.BR reload\-or\-restart ;
existing sessions survive.
.TP
.B 2. PAM
.IR /etc/pam.d/sshd ,
.IR sudo ,
.IR sudo\-i ,
when they load
.B pam_openbastion
(or
.BR pam_llng ):
restored from the oldest copy that does not, taken from
.I /var/backups/open\-bastion/<name>.orig
(debconf) and then
.I /var/backup/open\-bastion\-setup\-<timestamp>/
in chronological order. With no such copy, a generic stack for the
distribution family is written (Debian:
.BR common\-* ;
RHEL:
.BR password\-auth ,
.BR system\-auth ),
marked
.IR "# Restored by ob-uninstall" .
A
.I sudo\-i
that the setup created is removed instead. A debconf
.I .orig
that itself loads the module is removed, or the package's purge would copy it
back. On a dpkg host whose debconf
.B open\-bastion/pam\-mode
is not
.BR none ,
it is set to
.BR none :
otherwise the next
.B dpkg \-\-configure
of the package writes the module back into
.IR /etc/pam.d/sshd .
The previous value is saved in the backup as
.IR debconf\-selections .
.TP
.B 3. sudo
.I /etc/sudoers.d/open\-bastion
(only if it contains one of the two headers the setup writes: Mode E's, or
the backend's LLNG sudo rule), the
legacy
.IR ob\-bastion\-cert\-helper ,
and the group
.BR open\-bastion\-sudo .
.TP
.B 4. NSS
the
.B openbastion
source is removed from the
.BR passwd ,
.B group
and
.B shadow
lines of
.I /etc/nsswitch.conf
\(em an edit, not a restore, so later changes survive \(em and
.I nss_openbastion.conf
is removed. On an authselect host, where the setup had replaced the symlink
with a file, the symlink is put back instead when its target exists.
.TP
.B 5. units
.BR ob\-heartbeat.timer ,
.BR ob\-cert.socket ,
.BR ob\-record.socket ,
.BR ob\-fp.socket ,
.BR ob\-session\-prune.timer ,
.BR ob\-krl\-refresh.timer ,
.B ob\-audit\-rotate.timer
are disabled and stopped. The timers' schedule drop\-ins the setup wrote,
.IR /etc/systemd/system/<timer>.d/schedule.conf ,
are removed \(em only when their first line is
.IR "# Open Bastion timer schedule" ;
an administrator's drop\-in stays \(em and systemd is reloaded.
.TP
.B 6. opt\-in features
Hardening: the logind and limits drop\-ins;
.I /etc/at.allow
and
.I /etc/cron.allow
only if byte\-identical to the shipped templates; atd is unmasked if masked
(there is no telling whether an administrator masked it too); systemd\-logind
is reloaded, never restarted. Audit trace: the rules, then
.BR "augenrules \-\-load" .
Mode E:
.I /etc/ssh/revoked_keys
when nothing left in the sshd configuration refers to it. On a host still
running the KRL and audit cron jobs instead of the timers (see
.BR ob-post-upgrade (8)):
.IR /etc/cron.d/open\-bastion\-krl ,
.I /etc/cron.daily/open\-bastion\-audit\-rotate
(or its
.I cron.weekly
copy), and
.I /usr/local/bin/open\-bastion\-refresh\-krl
when it is the script the setup generated.
.TP
.B 7. generated files
The tmpfiles rule,
.IR openbastion.conf ,
.IR ssh\-proxy.conf ,
.IR session\-recorder.conf ,
.IR allowed_bastions ;
the contents of
.I /var/cache/open\-bastion
and
.IR /var/cache/nss_llng .
.TP
.B 8. server token
Its refresh token and its access token are revoked at the portal's
.I /oauth2/revoke
endpoint, then the token file is deleted. A confidential client authenticates
with a
.B client_secret_jwt
assertion built by
.BR ob-client-jwt (8);
the client secret itself is never sent, and when that helper is missing the
tokens are not revoked rather than sent without it. A public client sends its
client_id alone. Nothing sensitive is put on a command line. The portal
answers 200 even for a token it does not know, so success means the request
was accepted. Revocation is best effort: when it fails, the command says so
and carries on.
.SH SAFETY
.TP
.B Everything it touches is backed up first
to
.IR /var/backup/open\-bastion\-uninstall\-<timestamp>/ ,
mode 0700, under each file's full path. The uninstall can be undone by copying
the tree back. Two exceptions: the caches (derived data, holding encrypted
credentials) and the server token (a live credential), which are deleted
without a copy.
.TP
.B Session recordings are never deleted.
.I /var/lib/open\-bastion/sessions
is left as it is and its location printed at the end. Note that
.B apt purge open\-bastion
currently deletes
.I /var/lib/open\-bastion
with the recordings in it: move them first if you need them.
.TP
.B It refuses to lock you out.
Run through
.BR sudo (8)
by a user who looks like an SSO user of the host, it refuses unless
.B \-\-force
is given, and says which of these matched: not in
.IR /etc/passwd ;
a uid inside the NSS module's range
.RB ( min_uid .. max_uid
in
.IR nss_openbastion.conf ,
10000..60000 by default), which is where
.B create_user
puts SSO users it writes into
.IR /etc/passwd ;
a member of
.BR open\-bastion\-sudo ;
or no usable password in
.I /etc/shadow
and no
.IR ~/.ssh/authorized_keys .
Whoever runs it: afterwards, LemonLDAP::NG certificates are no longer
trusted and only local accounts can log in, through
.I authorized_keys
or a password as the restored sshd defaults allow. Keep console access.
.TP
.B The client secret is not copied.
The backup copy of
.I openbastion.conf
has its secrets (\fBclient_secret\fR and the other secret keys) replaced by
.IR "<redacted by ob-uninstall>" :
the client secret is the project's, shared by every host. The setup's
own backups keep it in clear; the summary lists them so they can be shredded.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
It does not delete recordings,
.I service\-accounts.conf
or
.I service\-accounts.d/
(operator data, inert once sshd and PAM are restored), SSO users' home
directories, or the package. It does not revert
.BR ob-desktop-setup :
a display\-manager stack that still loads
.B pam_openbastion
is listed and the exit status is 2. It does not touch the LemonLDAP::NG
Manager: delete the host's device entry there. Leave
.B pamAccessServerGroups
alone: it is keyed by the project's client_id, which every host of the project
shares. On a bastion, remove its id from the backends'
.IR allowed_bastions .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and change nothing. Reports the same refusals a real run would
hit. It runs without root, but then cannot read
.IR openbastion.conf ,
the sudoers drop\-in, the token or
.IR /etc/shadow ;
it lists what it could not read and says the plan is partial. Use
.B sudo ob\-uninstall \-\-dry\-run
for the full plan.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-revoke
Do not contact the portal. The token file is still deleted; revoke it, or
delete the host, in the LLNG Manager.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Proceed even when invoked by an SSO\-only user.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Usage.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
done, or nothing to do (the host was never set up)
.TP
.B 1
refused, aborted, or failed; the reason is on standard error
.TP
.B 2
done, but a stack under
.I /etc/pam.d
still loads
.B pam_openbastion
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/backup/open\-bastion\-uninstall\-<timestamp>/
this command's backup
.TP
.I /var/backup/open\-bastion\-setup\-<timestamp>/
the setup's backups (basenames only), read to restore the PAM stacks,
.I sshd_config
and the authselect symlink
.TP
.I /var/backups/open\-bastion/*.orig
the debconf backups of the PAM stacks
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OB_ROOT
For the test suite only: a directory prepended to every path, so a real
uninstall can run against a fake root. Setting it also skips the root check,
and keeps the caller's
.B PATH
(so the tests can substitute sshd, systemctl and the rest). Without it,
.B PATH
is set to
.IR /usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8),
.BR ob-post-upgrade (8),
.BR ob-krl-refresh (8),
.BR ob-bastion-id (1),
.BR ob-client-jwt (8)
